  "question": "User Management Module: Users register by providing information such as username, password, email, and phone number. The system assigns a default role to each user and records the user's registration time and update time. User information can be modified, viewed, or deleted. Each user includes the following Attributes: User ID (Primary key), username, password, email, phone number, role, registration time, and update time.\n\nCredit Management Module: Administrators can create and manage credit records for employees by filling in information such as credit number, credit type, application date, monthly factor, and number of applications. After each credit record is created, the system records the creation time and update time, and allows administrators to modify the credit information. Credit records can also be deleted, and the system records the deletion time. Credit records are associated with the following entities: user, employee, company, and payment. Each credit record includes the following Attributes: credit number (Primary key), credit type, application date, monthly factor, number of applications, creation time, update time, deletion time (nullable), User ID (Foreign key, associated with user table), Employee ID (Foreign key, associated with employee table), Company ID (Foreign key, associated with company table).\n\nCredit Record Status Management Module: The system can manage the status of credit records, and users and administrators can view the current status of credits (such as valid, invalid, terminated, etc.). Each time a credit record is updated, deleted, or activated, the system automatically records the timestamp and operator of the operation to support auditing and historical tracking. Credit record statuses include valid, invalid, terminated, etc.\n\nCompany Management Module: Administrators can manage company information, including company name and company address. Each company has the following Attributes: Company ID (Primary key), company name, company address, creation time, update time.\n\nEmployee Management Module: Administrators can manage employee information, including employee name, position, and affiliated company. Each employee has the following Attributes: Employee ID (Primary key), employee name, employee position, affiliated Company ID (Foreign key, associated with company table).",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0111\nCREATE TABLE `company` (\n  `company_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `company_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `company_address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`company_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `employee` (\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `employee_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_position` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `company_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`employee_id`),\n  KEY `employee_ibfk_1` (`company_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `employee_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`company_id`) REFERENCES `company` (`company_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `user` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(20) NOT NULL,\n  `role` varchar(50) NOT NULL DEFAULT 'user',\n  `registered_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `user_username_unique` (`username`),\n  UNIQUE KEY `user_email_unique` (`email`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `credit_record` (\n  `credit_number` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `credit_type` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `application_date` date NOT NULL,\n  `monthly_factor` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `application_count` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `deleted_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `employee_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `company_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `payment_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`credit_number`),\n  KEY `credit_record_ibfk_1` (`user_id`),\n  KEY `credit_record_ibfk_2` (`employee_id`),\n  KEY `credit_record_ibfk_3` (`company_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `credit_record_status_history` (\n  `status_history_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `credit_number` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `status` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `operation_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `operation_timestamp` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `operator_user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`status_history_id`),\n  KEY `credit_record_status_history_ibfk_1` (`credit_number`),\n  KEY `credit_record_status_history_ibfk_2` (`operator_user_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `credit_record_status_history_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`credit_number`) REFERENCES `credit_record` (`credit_number`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `credit_record_status_history_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`operator_user_id`) REFERENCES `user` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
